  "tables": [
    {
      "caption": "",
      "index": 0,
      "text": "As you work through the lab: Q#: Bold, numbered items reference things that need to be included in your lab report questions to answer, data, plots, or drawings ■ Items with a square bullet are action items you’ll need to do to progress through the lab. ➤ Items with an arrow are hints, tips, or things to keep in mind when completing tasks. Boxed information is about either the expectations for your lab reports or key concepts for the course."
    },
    {
      "caption": "",
      "index": 1,
      "text": "What is Uncertainty? Uncertainty is the acknowledgement that we cannot measure things exactly, and so any conclusion based on measurements will always include a range of possible answers. When making a measurement, there is no single “true value” of the measurand (measured quantity) that we can determine. Instead, we can define an interval that defines the range of values that we think are probable for the measurand. The narrower the interval, the more precisely we can approximate the measurand. When including uncertainty in a value, we report it as A ± ΔA. We believe the value is A, but we’re unable to rule out the possibility that it’s higher or lower by an amount ΔA. (Sometimes it’s written +Δ1 / -Δ2 if the higher and lower ranges are different.) For example, if we’re confident a time is between 2.42 and 2.48 s, it would be reported as 2.45 s ± 0.03 s. Common sources of uncertainty include: your judgement in reading analogue instruments the effects of environmental conditions on the measurement the sensitivity of your instruments (e.g. a digital scale, a meter stick) the rating or stated calibration of the instrument approximations and assumptions that you make while doing the experiment variations in repeated readings made under apparently identical conditions (statistical uncertainty)"
    },
    {
      "caption": "",
      "index": 2,
      "text": "Whenever you create a plot, you should: Not connect the data points with lines. Make sure you create Scatter Plots and not Line Plots. Evaluate if the plot makes sense. Are the axes correct? Do you have the right number of data points? Include a descriptive title and labels on each axis, always including physical units. Add a legend, unless you only have one set of data and the title describes it. Display the equation and R2 value for any trendline you include"
    },
    {
      "caption": "",
      "index": 3,
      "text": "Linearization In the context of data analysis, “linear” refers to a directly proportional relationship between the dependent and independent variables in an equation. Mathematically, if two variables 𝑥 and 𝑦 have a linear relationship, we can express this as 𝑦 = 𝑚𝑥 + 𝑏 where m and b are constants More simply, we can write this as 𝑦 ∝ 𝑥 where ∝ means “is proportional to”. When graphed, data with a linear relationship falls on a straight line. Linearization comes in handy when your data or hypothesis is not linear. When you fit data with a linear trendline, you are essentially checking whether the two variables are proportional to each other. To check a nonlinear hypothesis, can we instead check if one variable is proportional to a function of the other variable. In practice, linearization is done by calculating a new column of data, that gives the values of a function of the input variable. Typically, the input variable is the independent variable, and the function is chosen so that the resulting quantity is proportional to the dependent variable. Why Linearize? Linear data is typically easier to understand and analyze. There are analysis methods we can apply to linear data that don’t work as well (or at all) with nonlinear data. It is relatively easy to fit a line to data - there are just two important values to determine: the slope and intercept of the data."
    }
  ],
